$$-9x + 21y = k$$', NULL, $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"$-\\dfrac{63}{2}$"},{"key":"B","text":"$\\dfrac{63}{2}$"},{"key":"C","text":"$-21$"},{"key":"D","text":"$21$"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'For infinitely many solutions, the second equation must be a scalar multiple of the first. Comparing coefficients of $x$: $-9 / 6 = -3/2$. Checking $y$: $21 / (-14) = -3/2$. So the multiplier is $-3/2$, and $k$ must equal $-\\frac{3}{2} \\times 21 = -\\frac{63}{2}$. The distractor $\\frac{63}{2}$ results from applying the correct magnitude but dropping the negative sign. The distractor $-21$ results from assuming the constant must simply match the first equation''s constant with a sign change, without computing the actual scalar multiple. The distractor $21$ results from assuming the constants must be identical across both equations, ignoring the scaling factor entirely.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"sign_error"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"concept_gap"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 90, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-18"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-18","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);



